      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Patients with coronary heart disease may require a percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty, involving the use of a balloon catheter to dilate the arteries introduced via a sheath. It is now increasingly common for the removal of the sheath to be a nursing procedure. This paper reviews the literature, best practice, potential complications and post-removal nursing care."},
